# Copy to tools/.release-env and fill in. That file is gitignored.
#
#   cp tools/.release-env.example tools/.release-env
#
# tools/release.sh sources this, and Tauri reads all four during the build: it signs the .app
# with the identity, submits it to Apple's notary service, and staples the ticket before
# assembling the .dmg. The script then notarizes and staples the .dmg separately.
#
# Full one-time setup is in docs/notarization.md.

# The certificate's full common name, exactly as Keychain Access shows it.
# Find it with:  security find-identity -v -p codesigning
# release.sh auto-detects the first Developer ID Application cert if this is unset, but a bare
# `cargo tauri build` does not: it ad-hoc signs without warning. Set it, so the smoke test in
# docs/notarization.md signs the same way a release does.
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID1234)"

# The Apple ID on the Developer Program membership.
APPLE_ID="you@example.com"

# An APP-SPECIFIC password, not the Apple ID password.
# Generate at https://account.apple.com > Sign-In and Security > App-Specific Passwords.
# Format is xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx.
APPLE_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"

# The 10-character Team ID, from https://developer.apple.com/account under Membership details.
APPLE_TEAM_ID="TEAMID1234"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Mac App Store only
#
# tools/release-mas.sh reads these. The DMG path does not use them.
#
# Both certificates are found automatically in the keychain by their common names, so these two
# are only needed to disambiguate when more than one is installed. Note the second one's name:
# the developer portal calls it "Mac Installer Distribution", and no certificate's common name
# reads that.
# MAS_APP_IDENTITY="Apple Distribution: Your Name (TEAMID1234)"
# MAS_INSTALLER_IDENTITY="3rd Party Mac Developer Installer: Your Name (TEAMID1234)"

# App Store Connect API key, from Users and Access > Integrations > App Store Connect API.
# An API key is better auth for uploads than the app-specific password above, and it is what
# altool's --api-key / --api-issuer want.
#
# The private key file must be named AuthKey_<key id>.p8 and live in one of the directories
# altool searches:  ./private_keys  ~/private_keys  ~/.private_keys
#                   ~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys  $API_PRIVATE_KEYS_DIR
ASC_API_KEY_ID="ABC123DEF4"
ASC_API_ISSUER_ID="00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
